Research Project Idea: Behavioral, Physical, and Mental Health Across U.S. States

The goal of this research project is to examine relationships among behavioral
health risks, physical health, mental and emotional health, and healthcare
economic burden across U.S. states.

The project will use multiple public health datasets from the Centers for
Disease Control and Prevention (CDC).

The first dataset is the Smoking-Attributable Mortality, Morbidity, and
Economic Costs (SAMMEC) dataset, which provides estimates of healthcare
expenditures attributable to cigarette smoking across U.S. locations,
healthcare service categories, and years.

The second dataset is the CDC Nutrition, Physical Activity, and Obesity –
Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System dataset. It provides state-level
measures related to obesity, physical activity, nutrition, and demographic
characteristics.

The third dataset is the CDC Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System
(BRFSS) Prevalence Data. This dataset provides a wide range of health
indicators and will be used to identify measures related to mental and
emotional health, such as poor mental health and mental distress.

The main research objective is to investigate whether states with greater
behavioral and physical health risks also tend to experience poorer mental
health outcomes and greater healthcare-related economic burden.

Possible research questions include:

1. How do obesity, physical activity, and mental-health indicators vary
   across U.S. states?

2. Are states with higher obesity prevalence also associated with poorer
   mental or emotional health indicators?

3. Are behavioral and physical health indicators associated with differences
   in healthcare economic burden across states?

The project will attempt to compare or combine the datasets using common
geographic variables such as state or location. Differences in time coverage,
variable definitions, and data structure will need to be addressed during the
data-cleaning and integration stages.

The expected outcome is a state-level analysis that provides a broader view
of how behavioral, physical, mental, and economic health indicators may be
related across the United States.
